            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Stars are a polyvalent symbol depending strongly on context. They most prominently represent angels or heavenly beings, but are equally well-recognized as representing the righteous/glorified saints, the descendants of Abraham or twelve tribes of Israel, earthly rulers, or Christ.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Stars symbolize the righteous (who rule the night with the Son) and the twelve tribes of Israel (associated with the twelve constellations).\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"A polyvalent symbol primarily representing angels/heavenly beings, but also the righteous, Israel/the twelve tribes, earthly rulers, and Christ.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The righteous and the twelve tribes of Israel.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUBSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book restricts the meaning to the righteous and the twelve tribes of Israel, omitting the consensus primary identification of angels and heavenly beings, as well as other common symbolic referents like human rulers and Christ.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book's specific identifications are widely attested in the consensus as correct contextual readings (based on passages like Daniel 12:3 and Genesis 37:9); the disagreement is only in the book's exclusion of the other established meanings.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book selects well-recognized alternatives from the consensus (the righteous and the tribes of Israel) while ignoring the primary consensus meaning (angels). This narrows the polyvalent consensus to a specific subset of its established referents.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"By restricting the symbol to the righteous and the twelve tribes, the entry ignores explicit biblical texts that directly identify stars as angels or heavenly beings (e.g., Revelation 1:20; Job 38:7).\",\n  \"confidence